{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**The problem.** Implement a class `BSTIterator` over the in-order walk of a binary search tree: the constructor takes the `root`, `next()` returns the next smallest value, and `hasNext()` says whether any are left. The follow-up asks for `next()` and `hasNext()` in **O(1) amortized time** with **O(h) memory**."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/287c1ca2-cd64-455c-b506-abb639d0dddc.svg",
      "alt": "The tree 7, 3, 15, null, null, 9, 20. The iterator's stack starts with the left spine: 7, then 3 on top. next() pops 3. Then next() pops 7 and pushes the left spine of 7's right child: 15, then 9 on top. So the stack never holds more than one path from the root.",
      "width": 930,
      "height": 408,
